import type { BlogPost } from './types';

export const november06BlogPosts: readonly BlogPost[] = [{"slug":"qualify-inbound-leads-with-a-two-minute-fit-checklist","title":"Qualify inbound leads with a two-minute fit checklist","description":"A practical intake routine that helps a BDR team route inbound leads with consistent evidence instead of memory.","category":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/qualify-inbound-leads-with-a-two-minute-fit-checklist.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why a fast fit check protects the queue","paragraphs":["Inbound leads arrive through forms, event lists, referrals, and replies to outreach, and each source carries a different amount of context. Without a shared fit check, the first person who opens the lead decides from memory whether it deserves time. That decision is hard to audit and easy to reverse when a manager disagrees. A two-minute checklist gives everyone the same starting point and creates a record the team can review later. The checklist also gives the owner a way to compare routing decisions across weeks without reading every lead.","A fit check is not a full qualification call. It is a short filter that uses information already available in the lead record and one approved source. The check should confirm company type, role, need, timing signal, and any conflict that blocks outreach. If a field cannot be answered from the record, the assistant marks it unknown instead of guessing. The output is a routing decision with a reason, not a rewritten lead. When a field is missing, the record should show the gap instead of a plausible guess."]},{"heading":"Define the five fields that matter","paragraphs":["Choose fields the team can verify without paid tools or private inference. Company type, employee range, operating region, buyer role, and stated problem usually cover the first pass. Add a sixth field only when it changes a routing decision, such as an active compliance requirement or an existing partner relationship. Every field needs a written definition with an example and a counterexample so the assistant can apply it consistently. Review the definitions with the person who will apply them before the first live batch.","Avoid fields that invite speculation about budget or urgency. A lead form may ask about budget, but the answer is often incomplete or aspirational, so treat it as a signal rather than a rule. Instead, record what the person wrote and the date they wrote it. If the team needs a budget check later, the owner can ask a direct question on the call. The checklist should separate what is known from what still needs discovery. A short note on the source of each field makes the check easier to defend later."]},{"heading":"Separate disqualification from delay","paragraphs":["A lead can fail the fit check for a clear reason, or it can lack the information needed to decide. Those are different outcomes and should produce different actions. A clear mismatch closes with a short reason and an optional referral path. A missing field stays in a holding queue with a named owner and a follow-up date. Mixing the two creates a queue that looks clean while hiding unresolved work. The holding queue should be visible in the same view as the active queue.","Write the disqualification reasons before the checklist goes live. Examples include an unsupported region, a buyer role outside the approved profile, a request for a service the team does not offer, or an active conflict with an existing client. The assistant should choose from that list rather than write free text. Free text is useful for notes but poor for counting patterns or training a replacement. Add a review date to each reason so the list does not become a permanent archive."]},{"heading":"Put the checklist inside the intake step","paragraphs":["The checklist works best when it appears in the same place the lead arrives. If the assistant has to open a second document, the check becomes optional and eventually disappears during busy weeks. Put the fields in the intake form or the first review task, and make the routing decision a required field. The record should show who checked the lead, when, and which fields were missing. A required field is more reliable than a reminder in a team meeting.","Keep the instructions next to the fields instead of in a separate manual. A short hover note or a linked definition is enough for most cases. For edge cases, provide one example of a correct decision and one example of an escalation. The goal is to reduce the number of times someone has to ask a manager what the rule means during a live queue. When a rule changes, update the note beside the field on the same day."]},{"heading":"Review edge cases with the owner","paragraphs":["Set a weekly review of leads that were held or disqualified near the boundary. The owner should look for two patterns: cases that should have moved forward and cases that should have stopped earlier. Each pattern points to a different fix. A missed opportunity usually means the definition is too narrow, while a wasted call usually means the check is too permissive. Bring the actual lead records to the review so the discussion stays concrete.","Do not change the checklist after every disagreement. Collect the cases, identify the field or definition that caused the confusion, and make one change at a time. Record the change date and the reason in a short version note. That history helps the assistant understand why the rule exists and helps the owner defend the process when results are reviewed. One change per week is easier to test than a full rewrite of the checklist."]},{"heading":"Measure whether the checklist improves routing","paragraphs":["Track three simple counts each week: leads checked, leads routed to a call, and leads closed at intake. Add the number of held leads that later receive a decision. These counts do not prove revenue impact, but they show whether the queue is moving and whether the hold pile is growing. Review the counts alongside the owner's sample of decisions. The counts should be read with the queue depth so a quiet week is not mistaken for improvement.","Avoid ranking the assistant on conversion alone. A low conversion rate can reflect a strict and useful filter, while a high rate can reflect a queue full of easy leads. Pair the count with a quality sample that checks whether the reason matches the evidence. The combination gives a more honest view of whether the checklist is doing its job. A sample of ten decisions is enough to spot a pattern in most weeks."]},{"heading":"Keep the checklist short as the offer changes","paragraphs":["Offers, regions, and partner relationships change, and a checklist that never changes becomes a source of bad routing. Put a review on the calendar for the first week of each quarter. Ask the owner to confirm each field, remove fields that no longer change a decision, and update the examples. Keep the version note visible so the team knows which rules are current. The owner should sign the version note so the team knows who approved the change.","When the team adds a new service, test the checklist on ten recent leads before changing the live form. Look for cases where the new service creates a fit that the old rules would reject. Update the definitions and the disqualification list together. A small test costs less than a month of misrouted leads and gives the assistant a chance to practice the new rule. Keep the old version available until the new one has survived a full month."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How long should the first fit check take?","a":"Aim for two minutes using fields already in the record and one approved source. If it takes longer, the lead needs a research task rather than a faster check."},{"q":"What should the assistant do with a missing field?","a":"Mark it unknown, keep the lead in a holding queue, and assign a follow-up date. Do not infer the missing value from an unrelated detail."},{"q":"Who should change the checklist?","a":"The owner of the queue approves changes after a weekly edge-case review. Record the date and reason so the team knows which version is live."}],"internalService":"/services/account-mapping","internalSibling":"/blog/run-a-weekly-account-tier-review-that-changes-the-queue","source":{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"}},{"slug":"write-a-discovery-brief-your-bdr-team-can-reuse","title":"Write a discovery brief your BDR team can reuse","description":"A one-page brief format that gives a BDR the context to start a real conversation without turning research into a dossier.","category":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/write-a-discovery-brief-your-bdr-team-can-reuse.webp","sections":[{"heading":"What the brief must answer","paragraphs":["A discovery brief exists to help the BDR start a real conversation, not to prove how much research was done. It should answer four questions in one page: who the person is, why the account fits, what changed recently, and what the call needs to learn. Every other detail is optional and should be cut if it does not help the caller. The brief is a working document, so clarity matters more than completeness. The brief should also say what the caller should not raise on the first call.","The brief should also name the source of each important claim and the date it was checked. A claim without a source turns into a talking point the caller cannot defend. If the research found nothing recent, say so and explain where the caller should ask instead. Honest gaps are more useful than confident filler. A short note on the source date is enough to show when the evidence was last checked."]},{"heading":"Gather account evidence before the call","paragraphs":["Start with the company website, recent news, and the public profile of the person who booked the call. Then check the CRM for prior touches, open opportunities, and any support or billing issue that changes the context. The assistant should record the link and the access date for each source. This step usually takes less time than rewriting the same facts from memory. The research task should end when the brief has enough evidence, not when every tab is closed.","Limit the search to sources the team has approved. A public company page, a regulatory filing, a press release, and a professional profile are usually enough for a first call. Avoid speculation about internal plans or personal details that are not relevant to the conversation. The brief should help the caller ask a better question, not build a dossier. A source that cannot be linked should be described in one sentence with the date."]},{"heading":"Write the opening context in plain language","paragraphs":["Open the brief with two or three sentences the caller can read in ten seconds. State what the company does, the reason it may need the offer, and the specific trigger that led to the call. Use the same words a colleague would use in a hallway, not marketing language. The caller should be able to repeat the context without reading it aloud. The opening should avoid repeating the lead form in different words.","Keep the account description factual and short. Avoid adjectives that cannot be verified, such as fast-growing or innovative, unless the source supports them. If the trigger is an event, a hiring signal, or a public announcement, name it and link the source. The caller can then decide whether the trigger is strong enough to mention early. If the trigger is weak, say so and give the caller a question that tests it."]},{"heading":"List three questions tied to the offer","paragraphs":["Write three questions that connect the account context to the problem the offer solves. Each question should be open enough to invite a real answer and specific enough to test a hypothesis. For example, ask how the team currently handles a process, who owns the handoff, and what happens when the volume changes. Avoid questions that can be answered with a yes or no. A question that can be answered from the website is not a discovery question.","Put the most important question first because calls often run short. Add a note on what a good answer sounds like and what would change the recommendation. That note helps a new BDR recognize a fit signal without improvising. It also gives the reviewer a way to judge whether the brief prepared the caller for the conversation. The note on a good answer should be written by someone who has had the conversation."]},{"heading":"Add risks and open items","paragraphs":["List the risks that could make the opportunity a poor fit. A recent leadership change, a competing contract, a compliance restriction, or a mismatch in region can all change the next step. Mark each risk as confirmed or unverified. The caller should know which risks to raise directly and which ones to watch during the conversation. The risk list should be short enough to read before the call starts.","End with the open items that research could not resolve. Name the person who can answer each one and the best way to ask. This prevents the caller from treating an assumption as a fact. It also gives the assistant a clear follow-up task after the call instead of a vague instruction to learn more. Each open item should have an owner, even when the owner is the caller."]},{"heading":"Keep a reusable template with slots","paragraphs":["Build the brief as a template with fixed slots, short prompts, and a maximum length. Fixed slots make it easy to compare briefs and spot missing evidence. Short prompts tell a new assistant what to write without requiring a long style guide. A maximum length forces the writer to choose the facts that matter for the call. The template should fit on one page at the default font size.","Store the template with the team's other operating documents and link it from the intake task. Include one filled example and one example with a deliberate gap. The gap example is important because it shows that unknown is an acceptable answer. Review the template when the offer or the qualification criteria change. A filled example from a real call is more useful than a hypothetical one."]},{"heading":"Review the brief after the call","paragraphs":["Ask the caller to mark which parts of the brief were useful and which were wrong. A short note after the call is enough. Over time, those notes show which sources and fields deserve more attention. They also reveal when the research is too long for the time available or too thin to support the opening. The post-call note should take less time than the brief itself.","Use the feedback to change one part of the template at a time. If callers consistently ignore a section, remove it or replace it with a field they will use. If they consistently ask for missing context, add a prompt and an example. The brief should evolve with the calls it supports, not with the preferences of the person who wrote the template. A quarterly review of the template keeps the slots aligned with the current offer."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How long should a discovery brief be?","a":"One page is enough for most first calls. The caller needs context, three questions, and the open risks, not a complete account history."},{"q":"What if the research finds nothing recent?","a":"Say so in the open items and give the caller a specific question to ask. A documented gap is better than a paragraph of filler."},{"q":"How often should the template change?","a":"Change one part at a time after a pattern appears in post-call notes. Record the date and reason so the team can compare results."}],"internalService":"/services/meeting-coordination","internalSibling":"/blog/write-a-recovery-note-for-stalled-opportunities","source":{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"}},{"slug":"attach-prospect-research-evidence-to-the-crm-record","title":"Attach prospect research evidence to the CRM record","description":"A field-level method for keeping source links, dates, and confidence notes attached to the opportunity where the team can review them.","category":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/attach-prospect-research-evidence-to-the-crm-record.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why evidence gets lost","paragraphs":["Prospect research often lives in browser tabs, chat messages, and notes that never reach the CRM. The record then shows a stage and a next step but not the reason behind them. When the owner changes or the deal stalls, the team cannot tell which facts were verified and which were assumed. Attaching evidence to the record is the simplest way to make the work reviewable. The record should show the difference between a fact that was checked and a fact that was assumed.","Evidence does not mean a full research report. A source link, the date it was checked, and one sentence about what it supports are usually enough. The goal is to let a reviewer reconstruct the decision without contacting the person who did the research. That standard also makes the handoff to a new BDR much faster. A short evidence note is easier to maintain than a separate research folder."]},{"heading":"Pick one record as the source of truth","paragraphs":["Choose the CRM opportunity or account record as the place where evidence lives. Do not maintain a parallel spreadsheet unless it feeds the record through an approved process. A second list creates two versions of the truth and forces the team to reconcile them. If the CRM cannot store a link, use a structured note field with a consistent label. The record should be the one the account owner opens before a call.","Name the fields before the team starts adding content. A source URL, a source type, an access date, a supported claim, and a confidence label cover most cases. Add a reviewer field when someone other than the researcher needs to approve the entry. The field names should be short enough to use in a saved view and clear enough to train in one session. The field names should match the language the team already uses in review meetings."]},{"heading":"Store the source link and access date","paragraphs":["A link without a date is hard to trust because pages change, move, or disappear. Record the date the source was read and the date the claim was last confirmed. If the source is a document, save the title and version as well. The assistant should never paste a link that was forwarded without opening it. A link that requires a login should be labeled so the next reviewer knows the access limit.","Use the most direct source available. A company filing is stronger than a news summary, and a public pricing page is stronger than a third-party review. When only a secondary source exists, label it as secondary and note what it summarizes. That distinction helps the owner decide how much weight to give the evidence during a call. When a source disappears, keep the citation and note the date it was last available."]},{"heading":"Separate facts from inference","paragraphs":["Write the verified fact and the interpretation in separate fields. The fact might be that the company opened a new office, while the inference is that the team may need local support. Keeping them apart lets a reviewer accept the fact and challenge the inference. It also prevents a guess from being repeated as if it were confirmed. The fact field should be short enough to read in the CRM list view.","Use a simple confidence label such as confirmed, likely, or unverified. Define each label with an example so the team applies it the same way. A confirmed fact has a direct source, a likely fact has supporting context, and an unverified item is a hypothesis. The label should describe the evidence, not the researcher's enthusiasm. Confidence labels should be reviewed when a new researcher joins the team."]},{"heading":"Use a short evidence summary field","paragraphs":["The summary field should be readable in under thirty seconds. Start with the claim, add the source and date, and finish with the implication for the opportunity. For example, a summary might say that a new regional office was announced, cite the announcement, and note that the local team may need onboarding support. That structure is easy to scan and easy to audit. The summary should stand alone if the link is opened weeks later.","Avoid copying long passages from the source. A quotation is useful only when the exact wording matters, and it should stay short. Most records need a paraphrase and a link, not a transcript. If the source is sensitive, store the minimum needed and follow the team's privacy rules for access. A sensitive source should be summarized in a way that does not expose personal data."]},{"heading":"Review records that lack evidence","paragraphs":["Run a weekly check for opportunities that moved stages without a linked source. Start with the records closest to a proposal or a commitment. Ask the owner to add the missing evidence or explain why it cannot be added. A short exception note is better than a blank field because it preserves the reason for the gap. The weekly check should begin with records that carry a proposal date.","Do not treat every missing link as a failure. Some evidence comes from a call and cannot be linked, so the note should say who said it and when. The point is to distinguish a documented conversation from an unsupported assumption. That distinction is what makes the review fair to the researcher and useful to the owner. A missing note from a call should name the caller and the date."]},{"heading":"Handle stale and conflicting sources","paragraphs":["Sources age at different rates. A company address may stay stable for years, while a leadership page can change in a week. Set a review window by source type and flag entries that have passed it. When two sources conflict, keep both, note the conflict, and ask the owner which one to trust for the decision. The review window should be shorter for pages that change often.","Do not silently overwrite an old entry when new evidence appears. Add the new source, date it, and mark the older entry as superseded. That history shows how the team's understanding changed and protects the record if the decision is questioned later. It also gives the assistant a clear pattern to follow when the same conflict appears again. A superseded entry should stay visible to the reviewer who approved it."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the minimum evidence standard?","a":"A direct link, the date it was checked, and one sentence naming the claim it supports. Add a confidence label when the source is indirect."},{"q":"Should a call note count as evidence?","a":"Yes, when it records who said what and when. Mark it as a conversation rather than a public source."},{"q":"What should happen when sources conflict?","a":"Keep both entries, note the conflict, and ask the owner which source governs the decision. Do not delete the older evidence."}],"internalService":"/services/crm-opportunity-support","internalSibling":"/research/can-a-crm-stage-change-be-traced-to-its-supporting-evidence","source":{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"}},{"slug":"run-a-weekly-account-tier-review-that-changes-the-queue","title":"Run a weekly account tier review that changes the queue","description":"A short review routine that keeps account tiers tied to evidence, capacity, and the next action instead of habit.","category":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/run-a-weekly-account-tier-review-that-changes-the-queue.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why tiers drift","paragraphs":["Account tiers are useful only when they change what the team does next. In many teams, the tier becomes a label that is set once and rarely revisited. High-value accounts receive attention by habit, while a new opportunity with a strong signal waits behind an old list. A weekly review keeps the tier connected to the work. A tier that never changes is a label, not a decision tool.","The review should not become a second pipeline meeting. Its job is to confirm that the queue still reflects the best use of the next five days. That means checking a small number of accounts, making a few explicit changes, and recording why the changes happened. A short review that produces action is better than a long discussion that produces consensus. The review should end with a short list the assistant can act on the same day."]},{"heading":"Choose tier criteria you can verify","paragraphs":["Pick criteria that come from the CRM or a public source, not from a feeling about the account. Fit with the offer, current relationship strength, timing signal, and expected effort are usually enough. Define each criterion with a threshold or a clear example. If the team cannot agree on the definition, the tier will not survive the first disagreement. The criteria should be written where the assistant can see them while preparing the packet.","Keep the number of criteria small. Five is workable and ten is hard to apply consistently. Separate fit from timing so a strong account with no trigger does not outrank a good account with an urgent need. The review should be able to explain every tier change with one sentence and one source. A criterion that cannot be checked from the record should be removed or rewritten."]},{"heading":"Prepare the review packet","paragraphs":["The packet should arrive before the meeting and fit on one screen. Include the account name, current tier, proposed tier, the criterion that changed, and the next action. Add a short note for any account that moved more than one level. The owner should be able to scan the list and identify the three decisions that need discussion. The packet should show the evidence for the proposed change in one line.","The assistant can prepare the packet from approved fields and a saved view. Do not ask the assistant to recommend a tier without a rule. A recommendation is acceptable when it cites the criterion and the evidence. Otherwise, the assistant should present the facts and leave the judgment to the owner. A recommendation without a rule should be marked as a question for the owner."]},{"heading":"Run the meeting in three passes","paragraphs":["First, confirm the accounts that must move up because a trigger or relationship changed. Second, confirm the accounts that must move down because the signal faded or the effort no longer matches the return. Third, review the accounts that stay the same but need a different next action. This order prevents the meeting from spending all its time on the comfortable middle. The three passes should be timed so the meeting does not drift into account reviews.","Give each pass a time limit and stop when the limit is reached. Decisions that cannot be made in the room go to a named owner with a date. The assistant records the outcome in the same view used to prepare the packet. That closes the loop and gives the next review a clean starting point. The assistant should read back the decisions before the meeting ends."]},{"heading":"Record the queue changes","paragraphs":["Every tier change should produce a visible queue change. A move up might add a research task, a call, or a tailored brief. A move down might remove a task, change the follow-up interval, or close the account. If the tier changes but the queue does not, the review has not done its job. The queue change should be visible before the next work block starts.","Record the reason in a short field that can be counted later. Use a controlled list such as new trigger, relationship change, fit correction, or no response. The list does not need to be perfect at first. It needs to be consistent enough to show which reasons appear most often and where the criteria may be wrong. The reason list should be reviewed after a month to see which codes are unused."]},{"heading":"Watch for tier inflation","paragraphs":["Tier inflation happens when every account slowly moves up because no one wants to demote a relationship. The result is a top tier that contains most of the book and no longer guides attention. Set a target range for each tier and review the distribution at the end of the month. If the top tier is too large, the criteria or the review discipline needs adjustment. The distribution should be checked against the team's capacity, not only the account value.","Demotion is not a judgment about the relationship. It is a statement that the current evidence does not support the same level of effort. Explain that rule when the tier system is introduced. The owner should be able to move an account down without implying that the account is bad or that the previous decision was wrong. A demotion note should name the evidence that changed, not the person who missed a call."]},{"heading":"Keep the review short","paragraphs":["A weekly review should take less time than the work it directs. Prepare the packet in advance, limit the discussion to exceptions, and end with a written list of changes. If the meeting regularly runs long, reduce the number of accounts or move the detail to an async note. The review exists to protect attention, not to consume it. A review that runs long should be split into a short decision meeting and a separate deep dive.","Review the review after a month. Ask whether the tier changes led to different actions and whether the owner trusted the packet. If the team is not using the output, simplify the criteria or change the meeting format. A process that is easy to maintain is more likely to survive a busy quarter. The team should keep the packet format stable long enough to compare two months."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How many accounts should the weekly review cover?","a":"Cover the accounts that changed or need a decision, not the entire book. The packet can list the rest for context without discussing every row."},{"q":"What makes a tier criterion usable?","a":"A researcher can verify it from the CRM or an approved source, and two reviewers would apply it the same way to the same account."},{"q":"How do we prevent every account from becoming top tier?","a":"Set a target distribution and review it monthly. Require evidence for a move up and allow demotion without treating it as a relationship failure."}],"internalService":"/services/account-mapping","internalSibling":"/blog/qualify-inbound-leads-with-a-two-minute-fit-checklist","source":{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"}},{"slug":"set-human-approval-gates-in-a-partnership-outreach-sequence","title":"Set human approval gates in a partnership outreach sequence","description":"Where to pause an automated sequence so a person reviews commitments, sensitive replies, and relationship risks before a message goes out.","category":"Partnership systems","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/set-human-approval-gates-in-a-partnership-outreach-sequence.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Where automation needs a stop","paragraphs":["A partnership outreach sequence can prepare research, draft messages, and schedule follow-ups without much risk. The risk appears when a message creates an expectation, shares a commitment, or touches a relationship the team cannot repair automatically. Those moments need a human approval gate, not a faster queue. The sequence should show the gate in the same view as the message it blocks.","A gate is a deliberate pause with a named reviewer and a clear decision. It is different from a delay caused by an unavailable approver. The sequence should show what the reviewer needs to see, how long the review should take, and what happens when the reviewer is away. That design keeps the automation useful without hiding accountability. A reviewer who cannot see the evidence will approve the draft by default."]},{"heading":"Map the sequence before writing copy","paragraphs":["Write the sequence as a table with the trigger, the action, the evidence used, and the owner. Include the messages that are sent automatically and the ones that wait for review. Mark the points where a reply changes the direction of the conversation. The map should be short enough to review in one sitting and specific enough to test. The map should include the fields that must be complete before a message can be drafted.","Name the systems involved at each step. A CRM update, an email send, a calendar booking, and a task assignment may all have different owners. If a step depends on a field that is often missing, fix the field before adding the message. Automation built on incomplete data creates more review work than it removes. A missing field should stop the sequence rather than produce a generic message."]},{"heading":"Define the first approval gate","paragraphs":["The first gate usually sits before the first personalized message. The reviewer checks the account fit, the source of the personal detail, and the tone of the opening. The assistant should present the draft with the evidence beside it. A reviewer who has to open three other tabs will approve by habit instead of judgment. The first gate should also check that the person is the right contact for the offer.","Give the reviewer two clear options: approve, or return with a reason. Add an escalation path for a message that mentions pricing, legal terms, or a named partner. The reason codes should be short and stable so the team can see which drafts fail most often. That pattern is more useful than a free-text comment alone. A return reason should be specific enough to guide the next draft."]},{"heading":"Add a gate before shared commitments","paragraphs":["Any message that promises a meeting, a discount, a joint announcement, or an introduction needs a second gate. The reviewer should confirm that the person making the promise has the authority to keep it. The assistant can prepare the wording and the context, but it should not send the commitment. This is the line between support and representation. The authority check should name the person who can approve the commitment.","Keep a short list of commitment types that always require review. Update the list when a new partnership model appears. If a message falls outside the list but feels risky, the assistant should route it anyway. The rule should make escalation easy, not punish the assistant for being cautious. A cautious route should never be treated as a failure in the review."]},{"heading":"Route replies that change the relationship","paragraphs":["Some replies are routine and can be handled with an approved response. Others signal a change in interest, a complaint, a legal question, or a request for a senior contact. The sequence should classify those replies and route them to the owner. The assistant should not try to rescue a conversation that has moved outside the approved path. The routing rule should be visible in the inbox label, not only in the sequence settings.","Write the routing rules in plain language and include examples. A reply that asks for a contract, mentions a competitor's offer, or introduces a new stakeholder should go to the owner. A reply that asks for a calendar link can follow the standard path. The distinction should be visible in the inbox, not buried in a policy document. A reply that introduces a new stakeholder should go to the owner even when the tone is friendly."]},{"heading":"Audit gate bypasses","paragraphs":["Review a sample of sent messages each week and check whether each one passed through its required gate. Look for messages that were sent without a reviewer, reviews that took place after the send, and approvals with no evidence. Each finding points to a different problem in the sequence or the permissions. The sample should include messages from every sender and every active sequence.","Do not rely on the tool's log alone. Compare the log with the CRM activity and the reviewer's own record. If the three sources disagree, fix the integration before adding more automation. A gate that cannot be audited is only a suggestion, and a suggestion will be skipped when the queue is busy. A weekly check is enough when the sample and the fields stay consistent."]},{"heading":"Train the reviewer","paragraphs":["The reviewer needs practice with real drafts, not a slide about the policy. Use a set of ten examples that includes a clear approval, a clear rejection, and several edge cases. Ask the reviewer to explain the decision using the evidence. Compare the answers across reviewers and refine the examples where they disagree. The examples should include a message that looks personalized but uses no real evidence.","Reviewer training should be repeated when the offer, the partnership model, or the approval rules change. Keep the examples in a shared folder with the date and the reason for each update. A reviewer who understands the why behind the gate is more likely to apply it consistently when a new situation appears. A short refresher before a busy quarter is more useful than a long annual session."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Which messages need a human gate?","a":"Any message that creates a commitment, mentions pricing or legal terms, or changes the relationship should wait for a named reviewer."},{"q":"What should a reviewer see?","a":"The draft, the evidence behind any personal detail, the account context, and the reason for the outreach. Approval should not require opening several other tools."},{"q":"How do we know a gate was used?","a":"Compare the send log, the CRM activity, and the reviewer record. Repair the integration if the three sources disagree."}],"internalService":"/services/outreach-operations","internalSibling":"/blog/turn-event-attendee-lists-into-a-prioritized-follow-up-queue","source":{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}},{"slug":"write-a-recovery-note-for-stalled-opportunities","title":"Write a recovery note for stalled opportunities","description":"A short record that separates the last verified fact from the real blocker and forces one clear next action.","category":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"6 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/write-a-recovery-note-for-stalled-opportunities.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why stalled deals need a written recovery note","paragraphs":["A stalled opportunity is easy to leave in the pipeline because no one wants to close a relationship that might return. The record shows an old next step and a date that has passed. Without a written recovery note, the owner cannot tell whether the deal is waiting on the buyer, the team, or a missing piece of evidence. The note turns a vague hope into a decision. The note should be short enough to update during the same week the deal stalls.","The note is not a long postmortem. It is a short record of what was verified, what changed, and what the team will do next. It should be readable by someone who was not on the call. A good note makes it possible to reopen the opportunity with context or close it with a clear reason. A reader who was not on the call should be able to identify the next question."]},{"heading":"Establish the last verified fact","paragraphs":["Start with the most recent fact the team can defend. That might be a reply from the buyer, a signed document, a completed demo, or a clear statement that the timing has moved. Record the date and the source. If the last contact was a message with no reply, say that plainly instead of describing it as ongoing interest. The source should be a message, a document, or a dated note, not a memory.","Separate the last fact from the last activity. A CRM note may show a call, while the actual decision is still unknown. The recovery note should name the gap between the two. That gap is often the real reason the opportunity stalled, and it gives the owner a specific question to ask. The gap between activity and fact is often the most useful part of the note."]},{"heading":"Name the real blocker","paragraphs":["Use a short list of blocker types: no budget, no authority, no urgency, a competing priority, a missing stakeholder, or a product gap. Choose the one the evidence supports and explain why. If the evidence supports more than one, name the primary blocker and list the secondary one. A long list of maybes is not a diagnosis. The blocker list should be short enough to apply without a long discussion.","Avoid blaming the buyer for being unresponsive. The team may have chosen the wrong contact, sent an unclear next step, or failed to follow up at the right time. The note should describe the conditions that made progress hard, not assign fault. That framing makes it easier to change the approach on the next attempt. A note that blames the buyer usually hides a choice the team could have made differently."]},{"heading":"Choose one next action with a date","paragraphs":["A recovery note should end with one action and one date. The action might be a direct question to the buyer, an introduction to a new stakeholder, a revised proposal, or a decision to close. Assign an owner and a deadline. If the action depends on someone else, record the follow-up date for checking. The action should be something the team can start without waiting for a meeting.","Do not list five possible actions. That usually means the team has not decided what to learn next. Pick the action that would produce the most useful information in the shortest time. If the answer is that no action is justified, close the opportunity and record the reason. A closed record with a clear reason is better than a stalled record with none."]},{"heading":"Decide what to close","paragraphs":["Some opportunities should be closed even when the buyer is polite and the relationship is friendly. A deal with no budget, no authority, and no compelling event is not a forecast item. Closing it removes noise from the pipeline and frees attention for accounts with a real path. The note should make that decision explicit. The closing note should be visible to the owner and the manager who reviews the forecast.","Set a simple rule for how long an opportunity can stay stalled before a recovery note is required. The rule can be based on days since the last verified contact or days since the stage changed. Apply it consistently so the review does not depend on who remembers the deal. The stall window should be reviewed when the sales cycle changes."]},{"heading":"Hand the note to the owner","paragraphs":["The recovery note should be visible to the person who owns the relationship, not buried in a private task list. Put it in the opportunity record and tag the owner. If the owner is unavailable, name a backup. The note should include the next action and the date so the handoff does not require a meeting. The handoff should include the last verified fact, not only the next task.","When ownership changes, the new owner should be able to read the note and understand the history in a few minutes. That is the test of a useful record. If the note reads like a summary of every call, it is too long. If it reads like a status label, it is too short. A note that takes more than five minutes to read is too long for a handoff."]},{"heading":"Review recovery notes monthly","paragraphs":["Sample a set of recovery notes each month and look for patterns. Are most stalls caused by a missing stakeholder, a timing issue, or a weak follow-up? The pattern should change how the team qualifies and hands off new opportunities. A note that is never reviewed is only a record, not a learning tool. The monthly sample should include notes from different owners and account sizes.","Use the review to update the blocker list, the follow-up rules, and the training examples. Keep the changes small and dated. Over time, the team builds a shared language for why deals stall and what to do next. That language is more valuable than a single recovered deal. A change that is not dated is hard to connect to the results that follow."]}],"faq":[{"q":"When should a recovery note be written?","a":"Write one when an opportunity passes the team's stall window or when the next action has no date. Use the same window for every account."},{"q":"What if the buyer will not respond?","a":"Record the last verified fact, name the blocker, and choose one final action with a date. If the action produces no answer, close with a clear reason."},{"q":"How long should the note be?","a":"Short enough to read in a few minutes and specific enough for a new owner to act. One page is usually enough."}],"internalService":"/services/crm-opportunity-support","internalSibling":"/blog/attach-prospect-research-evidence-to-the-crm-record","source":{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"}},{"slug":"turn-event-attendee-lists-into-a-prioritized-follow-up-queue","title":"Turn event attendee lists into a prioritized follow-up queue","description":"A cleaning, scoring, and routing workflow for event contacts that keeps consent and capacity in view.","category":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/turn-event-attendee-lists-into-a-prioritized-follow-up-queue.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Start with the list you actually have","paragraphs":["Event attendee lists arrive in many formats: a badge export, a spreadsheet, a screenshot, or a set of business cards. Before scoring anyone, confirm the source, the date, and the fields the organizer is allowed to share. A list collected without consent may not be usable for outreach, even when the contact details are complete. The organizer should confirm in writing what the list may be used for.","Count the records and note how many have a company, a role, an email, and a stated interest. That first count tells you whether the list can support a queue or needs cleaning first. Do not promise a follow-up date until the usable volume is known. A small, clean list is better than a large one with missing fields. A list with many missing roles may need a cleaning step before any scoring."]},{"heading":"Normalize the attendee record","paragraphs":["Standardize company names, domains, and roles before scoring. Remove duplicates created by different badge spellings or personal email addresses. Decide how to handle attendees from the same company and whether each person gets a separate record. The rule should be written down so the next list is processed the same way. The cleaning rule should be stored with the import so the next event follows the same path.","Keep the original row and the cleaned row linked. If a field is corrected, record what changed and why. That audit trail matters when a contact disputes the source or when the team needs to explain how a person entered the queue. Cleaning should improve the record, not erase its origin. A corrected field should keep the original value in a separate column."]},{"heading":"Score fit before scoring interest","paragraphs":["Fit and interest are different signals and should be scored separately. Fit asks whether the attendee's company and role match the approved profile. Interest asks whether the person attended a relevant session, asked a question, or visited the booth. A strong fit with no interest may deserve a low-key follow-up, while weak fit with strong interest may need a qualification call. A high-interest contact with weak fit should go to a qualification call, not a warm sequence.","Use a small rubric with defined values. For example, score company fit from zero to two and interest from zero to two, then combine them into a routing band. Write the definition for each score next to the field. The assistant should be able to apply the rubric without asking for a judgment call on every row. The rubric should be tested on a small set before it is applied to the whole list."]},{"heading":"Write a follow-up angle per segment","paragraphs":["Group attendees by the reason they are likely to care about the offer. A session topic, a job role, a company type, or a stated problem can each define a segment. Write one follow-up angle per segment, not one per person. The angle should connect the event context to a specific question the team can ask. The angle should name the event context in the first sentence.","Keep the message short and free of claims the team cannot support. Mention the event, the relevant topic, and one reason for the follow-up. Do not pretend the team remembers a conversation that did not happen. If the attendee visited the booth, say so and include the note from that conversation. A booth conversation should be summarized in one line before the message is drafted."]},{"heading":"Assign owners and deadlines","paragraphs":["Every segment needs an owner and a first-touch deadline. The deadline should reflect the value of the event and the shelf life of the context. A conversation about an urgent problem may need a follow-up within a few days, while a general list can wait longer. Put the deadline in the queue, not in a planning document. The deadline should be visible in the same queue the owner works from.","Balance the work across owners before the queue goes live. Count the records in each segment and compare the load. If one owner receives most of the high-effort follow-ups, rebalance or extend the deadline. A queue that ignores capacity will produce late messages and uneven quality. A capacity check before launch prevents a backlog that looks like poor follow-up."]},{"heading":"Respect consent and contact rules","paragraphs":["Check the event's privacy notice and the team's own contact policy before sending anything. Some lists permit a single follow-up related to the event but not a general newsletter. Others require a specific opt-in. The assistant should know which rule applies and record the basis for the contact. The policy basis should be recorded in the contact record, not only in the import file.","Honor opt-outs immediately and keep a suppression list that is checked before every send. Do not move a contact to a different sequence because they declined one message. The rules should be visible in the workflow, not only in a legal document. A simple checkbox and a dated note prevent most mistakes. A monthly check of the suppression list catches gaps before the next event."]},{"heading":"Measure the queue after two weeks","paragraphs":["Review the queue two weeks after the event. Count how many records were contacted, how many replied, and how many moved to a real conversation. Also count the records that were closed because the fit was wrong or the contact asked not to be contacted. Those counts show whether the scoring was useful. The counts should be split by segment so a strong result is not hidden by a weak one.","Do not judge the event only by reply rate. A low reply rate can be acceptable if the list was small and the conversations were strong. A high reply rate can hide a queue that ignored fit. Use the counts to improve the next list, the rubric, and the follow-up timing. One change for the next event is enough to test whether the lesson was learned."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Can we email everyone who attended?","a":"Only when the event notice and the team's policy allow it. Record the basis for contact and honor any opt-out immediately."},{"q":"How should fit and interest be combined?","a":"Score them separately, then use a written routing band. Do not let a strong conversation override a clear fit failure without an owner review."},{"q":"How soon should follow-up start?","a":"Set the deadline by segment and event value. Put the date in the queue and balance the load before the first send."}],"internalService":"/services/market-research","internalSibling":"/blog/set-human-approval-gates-in-a-partnership-outreach-sequence","source":{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Census Business Builder","url":"https://www.census.gov/data/data-tools/cbb.html"}},{"slug":"teach-a-bdr-assistant-your-ideal-customer-profile-in-one-workshop","title":"Teach a BDR assistant your ideal customer profile in one workshop","description":"A working session that turns a profile document into observable traits, scored examples, and escalation rules.","category":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","readTime":"7 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/teach-a-bdr-assistant-your-ideal-customer-profile-in-one-workshop.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why a document alone fails","paragraphs":["An ideal customer profile document describes the kind of account the team wants, but it rarely teaches a new assistant how to recognize one in a live queue. The document may use words like enterprise, strategic, or high intent without showing what those words look like in a record. A workshop closes that gap by working through real examples. The workshop should produce a rule the assistant can use without opening the profile document.","The workshop should produce a shared decision rule, not a longer document. The team leaves with a small set of observable traits, a scoring method, and examples of accounts that pass and fail. The assistant should be able to apply the rule to a new record without asking for a manager's opinion on every row. A rule that requires a manager for every row is not a rule yet."]},{"heading":"Bring real examples to the workshop","paragraphs":["Select ten accounts that represent the range of the team's book: strong fits, weak fits, and a few edge cases. Remove sensitive details and use the same fields the assistant will see in the CRM. Include the outcome if it is known, but do not let the outcome define the fit score. The goal is to test the criteria, not to relitigate old deals. The examples should include an account that looked strong but failed the fit check.","Ask the owner to explain the decision on each account in one sentence. Record the reason and the trait it references. When two owners disagree, stop and define the trait more precisely. The disagreement is useful evidence that the profile needs a sharper rule. A disagreement between owners is a signal that the trait needs a sharper definition."]},{"heading":"Define fit with observable traits","paragraphs":["Each trait should be something a researcher can see in an approved source or a CRM field. Company size, industry, operating region, technology, buyer role, and a recent trigger are common examples. Avoid traits that require guessing intent or reading a person's mind. If a trait cannot be observed, it belongs in a discovery question, not the fit score. The trait should be visible in a field the assistant can actually open.","Write a positive and a negative example for each trait. The negative example is often more useful because it shows where the boundary sits. For size, for example, the team might define a range and explain what happens just outside it. The assistant can then apply the rule without interpreting vague language. The negative example should sit beside the positive one in the guide."]},{"heading":"Show the assistant where to look","paragraphs":["Walk through the exact sources the assistant should use for each trait. Name the CRM field, the public page, or the approved database. Show what a complete entry looks like and what a missing entry looks like. If a source is unreliable, say so and provide an alternative. The walkthrough should include one source that is unreliable and one that is approved.","Explain the order of the search. Start with the fields already in the record, then move to the approved public sources, then stop and mark the gap. This order prevents the assistant from spending an hour on an account that should have been routed in five minutes. It also makes the research consistent across the team. The search order should stop when the approved sources are exhausted."]},{"heading":"Practice with a scored set","paragraphs":["Give each participant the same five accounts and ask them to score the fit using the draft rule. Compare the scores and discuss every difference. If the difference comes from a missing definition, fix the rule. If it comes from a judgment the assistant should not make, add an escalation path. The practice set should include an account with a missing field.","Repeat the exercise with a new set after the rule is updated. The goal is not perfect agreement on every edge case. It is a shared understanding of the common cases and a clear path for the rest. Document the final rule and the examples in a short guide. The final guide should be short enough to keep beside the queue."]},{"heading":"Write the escalation rules","paragraphs":["The assistant needs to know when to stop. A new market, a public controversy, a named competitor, or a request from an executive may all require a human review. Write those triggers in plain language and place them next to the scoring rubric. The assistant should escalate early rather than force a fit. The trigger list should be reviewed when a new market or offer appears.","Name the person who receives each escalation and the expected response time. If the owner is away, name a backup. An escalation path without a backup becomes a queue of its own. Review the triggers after the first month and remove any that never produce a useful decision. A backup name turns an escalation path into a working route."]},{"heading":"Review the profile every quarter","paragraphs":["The ideal customer profile should change when the offer, the market, or the team's capacity changes. Put a quarterly review on the calendar and bring the latest wins, losses, and edge cases. Ask which traits still predict a good conversation and which ones have become noise. Update the examples as well as the criteria. The review should bring the accounts that were hardest to score, not only the wins.","Keep a version note with the date and the reason for each change. The assistant should be able to see which rule was in force when an account was scored. That history makes the profile easier to improve and easier to defend when someone asks why an account was routed a certain way. A date and a one-line reason are enough for the version note."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How many accounts should the workshop use?","a":"Ten is enough to cover strong fits, weak fits, and edge cases without turning the session into a full audit."},{"q":"What makes a trait observable?","a":"A researcher can confirm it from a CRM field or an approved public source. Intent and personal motives are discovery questions, not fit traits."},{"q":"How often should the profile change?","a":"Review it quarterly and after a major offer change. Keep a dated version note so old scores can still be explained."}],"internalService":"/services/market-research","internalSibling":"/blog/write-a-discovery-brief-your-bdr-team-can-reuse","source":{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"}}];
